        <body>
  <h1>Student Rights & Discipline in District 26: A Parent's Guide to Manifestation Determinations and Due Process</h1>

  <p>Welcome to a comprehensive guide regarding student rights and discipline procedures within NYC's District 26, which includes the neighborhoods of Bayside, Douglaston, Little Neck, Fresh Meadows, Oakland Gardens, and Glen Oaks. It's crucial for students and parents, especially those with children receiving special education services, to understand these processes to ensure fair and equitable treatment within our schools. This guide focuses on disciplinary procedures, particularly concerning students with Individualized Education Programs (IEPs) and 504 Plans.</p>

  <h2>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): A Deep Dive</h2>
  <p>A Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is a critical safeguard designed to protect students with disabilities from being unfairly disciplined for behaviors related to their disability. According to the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Part 200 Regulations, a Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is triggered when a student with an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or a 504 Plan faces a suspension exceeding ten consecutive school days, or a series of shorter suspensions that constitute a "pattern" resulting in a change of placement. What constitutes a "pattern" is not explicitly defined, but generally, a pattern is found when a student has been suspended multiple times for similar behaviors. The MDR is essentially a meeting to determine whether the student's misconduct is a direct result of their disability or the school's failure to properly implement the IEP.</p>

  <p><b>Key Participants in the MDR Process:</b> The MDR team *must* include the parent(s), relevant members of the IEP team (including the student, when appropriate), and qualified school personnel familiar with the student and the disciplinary situation. This often includes the student's special education teacher, general education teacher (if applicable), a school psychologist or counselor, and a school administrator.</p>

  <p><b>The Two-Pronged Test of the MDR:</b> During the MDR meeting, the team addresses two critical questions, as outlined by IDEA:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Was the conduct in question caused by, or did it have a direct and substantial relationship to, the child's disability?</li>
    <li>Was the conduct in question the direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP?</li>
  </ol>

  <p>If the answer to *either* of these questions is "yes," then the behavior is deemed a manifestation of the student's disability. It's important to understand that the burden of proof lies with the school district to demonstrate that the behavior was *not* a manifestation of the disability.</p>

  <p><b>What Happens if the Behavior is a Manifestation of the Disability?</b> If the MDR team determines that the behavior *is* a manifestation of the disability, the disciplinary action must cease. The IEP team *must* then immediately conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted. An FBA is a process of gathering information about the student's behavior, including the triggers, the behavior itself, and the consequences, to understand the function or purpose of the behavior. Based on the FBA, the team must then develop or revise a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) to address the problematic behavior. The BIP should include proactive strategies to prevent the behavior from occurring, as well as reactive strategies to manage the behavior when it does occur.</p>

  <p><b>What Happens if the Behavior is *Not* a Manifestation of the Disability?</b> If the MDR team determines that the behavior is *not* a manifestation of the disability, the student can be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities. However, the school district must still provide Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) to the student during the period of suspension. This often means providing alternative educational services, such as tutoring or home instruction, to ensure that the student continues to make progress on their IEP goals.</p>

  <p><b>Parent Rights During the MDR Process:</b> Parents have the right to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Receive timely notification of the MDR meeting.</li>
    <li>Review all relevant records and information prior to the meeting.</li>
    <li>Actively participate in the MDR meeting and provide their perspective.</li>
    <li>Bring an advocate or attorney to the MDR meeting.</li>
    <li>Disagree with the MDR determination and challenge it through due process (more on this later).</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>District 26 Specific Considerations:</b> District 26, like all NYC school districts, must adhere to Chancellor's Regulation A-832 regarding student discipline. This regulation outlines the procedures for suspensions, including the rights of students and parents. Be sure to familiarize yourself with A-832, available on the NYC Department of Education website.</p>

  <h2>Advice for Suspensions in District 26: A Step-by-Step Guide for Parents</h2>
  <p>If your child receives a suspension notice in District 26, it's a stressful situation, but understanding your rights and taking the right steps is crucial. Here's a detailed guide:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><b>Review the Suspension Letter Carefully:</b> The suspension letter should clearly state the reason for the suspension, the length of the suspension, and your right to appeal the suspension. Note the date and time of the incident, and the specific rule that your child is alleged to have violated.</li>
    <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep a detailed record of all interactions with school officials, including the date, time, who you spoke with, and what was discussed. This documentation can be invaluable if you need to challenge the suspension.</li>
    <li><b>Request a Meeting with School Administrators:</b> Schedule a meeting with the principal or assistant principal to discuss the incident. Be prepared to present your perspective and any evidence that supports your child's version of events. Bring copies of your child's IEP or 504 Plan, if applicable.</li>
    <li><b>Understand the MDR Process (If Applicable):</b> If your child has an IEP or 504 Plan and the suspension is for more than ten days (or a pattern), ensure that the school schedules a Manifestation Determination Review. Actively participate in the MDR, providing information and insights about your child's disability and how it may have contributed to the behavior. Don't hesitate to ask questions and challenge any assumptions made by the school.</li>
    <li><b>Consider Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</b> If you disagree with the school's evaluation of your child's behavior or disability, you have the right to request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense. An IEE can provide a second opinion and potentially strengthen your case. Note: The school district can challenge your request for an IEE, but they must initiate due process to do so.</li>
    <li><b>Appeal the Suspension (If Necessary):</b> If you disagree with the suspension decision, you have the right to appeal it. The suspension letter should outline the appeals process, including the timeline and the person to whom you should submit your appeal.</li>
    <li><b>Seek Legal Counsel:</b> If you believe your child's rights have been violated, or if you are facing a particularly complex disciplinary situation, consult with an attorney specializing in education law. They can advise you on your legal options and represent your child's interests.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Understanding Due Process Rights in District 26</h2>

  <p>Due process is a legal mechanism that allows parents to resolve disputes with the school district regarding their child's education. It is a formal process that involves mediation, a hearing, and potentially an appeal. You can initiate due process if you disagree with the school district's decisions regarding your child's identification, evaluation, placement, or the provision of FAPE.</p>

  <p><b>Common Reasons to File for Due Process:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Disagreement with the IEP: You disagree with the goals, services, or placement outlined in your child's IEP.</li>
    <li>Failure to Provide FAPE: The school district is not providing your child with the Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) to which they are entitled.</li>
    <li>Disciplinary Actions: You believe your child was unfairly disciplined, or that the school district violated their rights during a disciplinary process (e.g., MDR).</li>
    <li>Evaluation Disputes: You disagree with the results of the school district's evaluations and believe your child needs additional or different evaluations.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>The Due Process Process in New York State:</b></p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Filing a Due Process Complaint:</b> The first step is to file a written due process complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED). The complaint must contain specific information, including the child's name, address, school, a description of the problem, and the proposed resolution.</li>
    <li><b>Resolution Session:</b> Within 15 days of receiving the due process complaint, the school district must convene a resolution session with the parents. The purpose of the resolution session is to attempt to resolve the dispute informally.</li>
    <li><b>Mediation:</b> If the dispute is not resolved during the resolution session, the parties can agree to participate in mediation. Mediation is a voluntary process in which a neutral third party helps the parties reach a mutually agreeable solution.</li>
    <li><b>Due Process Hearing:</b> If mediation is unsuccessful, or if either party declines to participate, a due process hearing will be held. The hearing is conducted by an impartial hearing officer (IHO) who is appointed by NYSED. Both parties have the right to present evidence, call witnesses, and cross-examine opposing witnesses.</li>
    <li><b>IHO Decision:</b> The IHO will issue a written decision within 45 days of the hearing. The decision is binding on both parties, unless appealed.</li>
    <li><b>Appeal:</b> Either party can appeal the IHO's decision to the State Review Officer (SRO) within 30 days. The SRO will review the record of the hearing and issue a written decision. The SRO's decision is final and binding, unless appealed to a court of law.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Important Considerations for Due Process:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Timelines:</b> Due process has strict timelines. It is crucial to adhere to these timelines to protect your rights.</li>
    <li><b>Legal Representation:</b> Due process can be a complex legal process. It is highly recommended that you seek legal representation from an attorney specializing in special education law.</li>
    <li><b>Evidence:</b> You will need to gather and present evidence to support your case. This may include documents, evaluations, and witness testimony.</li>
    <li><b>Stay Put:</b> During the due process proceedings, your child has the right to remain in their current placement (often referred to as "stay put"). This means that the school district cannot change your child's placement without your consent or an order from the IHO.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common District 26 Pitfalls to Watch Out For</h2>

  <p>While District 26 aims to provide excellent services, here are some potential challenges parents may encounter and how to address them:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Delay in Evaluations:</b> Schools may delay initial evaluations or re-evaluations, impacting timely IEP development.
        <b>Solution:</b> Document all requests in writing. If delays persist, file a formal complaint with the NYCDOE or consider pursuing mediation.</li>
    <li><b>Inadequate IEP Goals:</b> IEP goals may not be specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART).
        <b>Solution:</b> Advocate for SMART goals during IEP meetings. If necessary, request an IEP amendment meeting or consider an IEE to provide additional data.</li>
    <li><b>Lack of Related Services:</b> The IEP may not include all necessary related services (e.g., speech therapy, occupational therapy, counseling).
        <b>Solution:</b> Present documented needs from medical professionals and therapists to support the inclusion of these services in the IEP.</li>
    <li><b>Resistance to Assistive Technology:</b> The school may resist providing necessary assistive technology devices or training.
        <b>Solution:</b> Conduct independent research on assistive technology options. Request an assistive technology evaluation. Cite IDEA regulations regarding assistive technology.</li>
    <li><b>Inconsistent Implementation of IEP:</b> The IEP is not consistently followed in the classroom.
        <b>Solution:</b> Document instances of non-compliance. Communicate with the teacher, special education coordinator, and principal. If the problem persists, consider filing a complaint or seeking legal assistance.</li>
    <li><b>Pressuring Parents to Change Placement:</b> Schools might try to pressure parents into accepting a less restrictive (or more restrictive) placement without proper justification.
        <b>Solution:</b> Know your child's rights regarding placement. Placement decisions MUST be based on the child's individual needs and the IEP. Never feel pressured to make a decision without careful consideration and consultation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) about Special Education & Discipline in District 26</h2>

  <dl>
    <dt><b>Q: What is FAPE?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> FAPE stands for Free Appropriate Public Education. It means that all children with disabilities are entitled to a public education that is tailored to their individual needs and provided at no cost to their parents.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: What is an IEP?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> IEP stands for Individualized Education Program. It is a written document that outlines a child's special education needs, goals, and services. It is developed by a team that includes the parents, teachers, and other relevant professionals.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: What is a 504 Plan?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> A 504 Plan is a plan developed for students with disabilities who do not require specialized instruction but need accommodations to access the general education curriculum. It is based on Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: Can my child be suspended for behavior related to their disability?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> Not without a Manifestation Determination Review. If the behavior is determined to be a manifestation of the disability, the disciplinary action must cease, and the IEP team must develop or revise a Behavior Intervention Plan.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the school's decision regarding my child's special education services?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> You have the right to challenge the school's decision through due process. This involves mediation, a hearing, and potentially an appeal.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: Where can I find more information about special education rights in New York State?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> You can find more information on the New York State Education Department (NYSED) website, as well as from organizations like the INCLUDEnyc and the Advocates for Children of New York.</dd>
  </dl>

</body>
